{"name":"Semi Opal","hex":"#ab9649","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"semi","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A semi-detached house.","A semi-trailer; a tractor-trailer; an eighteen-wheeler; an artic."]},{"part":"Adverb","definitions":["Somewhat; to some extent."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/semi"},{"term":"opal","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A mineral consisting, like quartz, of silica, but inferior to quartz in hardness and specific gravity, of the chemical formula SiO2·nH2O.\n A precious gem, an iridescent gemstone found in the opal-silica mineral substrate (potch)","A precious gem, an iridescent gemstone found in the opal-silica mineral substrate (potch)"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/opal"}],"entries":[{"title":"Opal","blocks":[{"text":"Opal is a hydrated amorphous form of silica (SiO2·nH2O); its water content may range from 3% to 21% by weight, but is usually between 6% and 10%. Due to the amorphous (chemical) physical structure, it is classified as a mineraloid, unlike crystalline forms of silica, which are considered minerals. It is deposited at a relatively low temperature and may occur in the fissures of almost any kind of rock, being most commonly found with limonite, sandstone, rhyolite, marl, and basalt."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Opal-53714.jpg/330px-Opal-53714.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Opal"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Opal_molecular_structure2.jpg/500px-Opal_molecular_structure2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Opal_molecular_structure2.jpg/1280px-Opal_molecular_structure2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/w